WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.760 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  HTC vive oculus rift and PlayStation VR  brought us virtual reality gaming back  in 2016 while software has matured at a  steady pace over the last two years VR  Hardware has remained the same for the  most part until now strapping on the  ergonomically refined HTC vive pro opens  your eyes to a perceptible step forward  and visual fidelity for vr gaming  unfortunately these enhancements come at  a hefty price that make it a product  reserved for the most demanding VR  enthusiasts what makes the vive pro so  noteworthy is the higher resolution of  its dual oled displays going from the  combined resolution of 2160 by 1200 on  the original HMD to 2880 by 1600 on the  vive pro significantly refines the  visual quality of any vr game the simple  bump and resolution translate to a much  sharper image small details like foliage  vegetation and tree leaves look like  fitting pieces to a graphical puzzle  rather than fuzzy textures that fill up  a space which was immediately noticeable  in skyrim vr and when a game environment  heavily relies on a number of these  objects to create a more lively and lush  world the absence of jaggies is both  immediately apparent and much  appreciated even the barren wasteland of  fallout vr benefits from the resolution  bump as building interiors and abandoned  towns felt a little bit less artificial  by swapping between the original vibe  HMV and the vive pro multiple times in  different scenarios for each game we  played the improvement was evident in  game text is also much more clear  navigating menus and reading prompts is  significantly easier as you spend less  time trying to get the right focus on  text in virtual reality by nature of VR  projecting images so close to your eyes  graphical blemishes come to the  forefront so for the vive pro to reduce  aliasing to great effect alleviate  similar flaws again these enhancements  are due to the increased resolution  don't expect better textures lighting or  character models of course that's not  what the vive pro is here for now a  higher resolution isn't without its  drawbacks though like with any game  increasing resolution is going to demand  more performance from your PC with vive  it's absolutely crucial to maintain the  90 frames per second that the 90 Hertz  displays offer and because of this HTC  raised the minimum and  recommended PC specs to use the vibe Pro  when it comes to your GPU  you'll need at least a GTX 1060 or RX  480  but the recommendation for an optimal  experience is either gtx 970 or RX vega  56 while we spent most of our time on  the vive pro using a system equipped  with the top-end gtx 980ti  we tested the new hmd with a 6 gigabytes  1060 to make sure the minimum specs is  enough for games like fallout 4 VR sadly  even with the graphics settings turned  down we often had missed frames  throughout our time playing it monitored  via steams display frame timings we  didn't have this issue with skyrim VR on  medium settings on the native resolution  raw data ran just fine with a few miss  frames on rare occasions and didn't  compromise the experience but you should  really have the recommended specs or  higher to get the most out of the vive  Pro a comfortable frame rate is one  thing physical comfort is another and  the vive pro stock head strap is on  point with a form-fitting face pad  around the HMD and dense foam on the  back of the strap the vive pro provides  enough comfort for long sessions also  it's seamless design has the head strap  moving as one piece the top has an  adjustable velcro strap while the back  consists of an easy-to-use dial that  loosens and tightens the whole thing now  only does the design make it simple to  put on and take off but it provides  impressive weight distribution no longer  does HTC's headset have that nagging  front heavy feel in addition to the  impressive comfort the head strap comes  with built-in on ear headphones these  don't exactly have the clarity of  high-grade audio equipment but they pack  enough thumping bass and there's minimal  distortion for high and mid frequencies  accurate positional audio is also a  highlight which is a necessity for the  VR experience one drawback is that the  headphones won't sit perfectly flush on  everyone's ears and can compromise sound  isolation now one of the most  intimidating factors with VR is the cost  of entry price drops from all the  leading platforms have certainly lowered  the barrier but unfortunately the vive  pro abandons this trend entirely for 800  bucks you're only getting the upgraded  HMD with the new breakout box that's it  the vibe Pro does not come with the vive  controllers or the two required base  station sensors those who have yet to  hop on the vive eco system will be  shelling out a pretty penny for the  necessary equipment  this essentially posits the vive pro  solely as an up  great for those who already have the  original headset even then the price tag  is a lot to ask  with the vive pro HTC is raising the bar  for vr gaming headsets the benefits of  its higher resolution are undeniable  along with an improved head strap for  better comfort and weight distribution  it seems the vibe Pro is a clear win for  HTC however the high price may make it  cost prohibitive for VR newcomers  especially because of the incomplete  package this is very much for the VR  enthusiast with a high-end PC who wants  the latest and greatest Hardware  regardless of the price tag it's further  enforced by the fact that the minimum  specs won't cut it for more demanding  games if you don't fall into this  category there isn't enough to warrant a  vive pro as it stands as great as the  vive pro is you won't miss out on any  games or functions that you don't  already get with the original 5  [Music]

